Apply order 0033 against mariopartyrd/partyboard 98ac07fd1e8f7ab938bbb6fe75789fad99419010 (CC0-1.0; ADR 0009 section 5).
hsfdraw, EnvelopeExec, m442Dll: inverse matrices that reach game memory start as zero, not uninitialised stack, on MPT_PC
Applied in order by runtimes/gc_wii/mp4/CMakeLists.txt with `git apply`.

M4-03, the MTXInverse audit (the patch 0029 pattern; docs/titles/mp4.md section 20). MTXInverse and
MTXInvXpose return 0 and leave the output untouched when the matrix is singular, which a model or a
bone scaled to 0 makes it. Of the pin's call sites, three put an output that may be uninitialised
into game memory:
- hsfdraw.c objMesh, the billboard paths: mtx is set first only on the non-envelope path of a
  model whose transform is applied, and MTXConcat stores it into DrawObjData's matrix and, by
  MTXCopy, the object's constant data.
- EnvelopeExec.c SetEnvelop, the multi-weight loop: sp170 is set first only by the single-weight
  loop, and MTXMultVec takes the normals through it into the envelope's normal buffer (sp110 is
  cleared with it: the dual-weight path's inverse of a unit-scale matrix sets it every time, but
  it costs nothing to be sure).
- m442Dll main.c fn_1_5EDC, a model hook: MTXConcat stores spC into the hook's matrix, which is
  DrawObjData's.
On PC the stale stack holds host frames, so these bytes would differ by code layout and run (the
layout gate's seed 1 writer, patch 0029, was the same shape). Every other site either inverts a
camera matrix (never singular), writes a persistent or already-set matrix, or only loads the result
into GX (normal and texture matrices, not game state); the list is in docs/titles/mp4.md. Locals are
zeroed at entry, so a path that set them first behaves as before. Unconditional on PC.

